.TH SANBOX 1 "June 2026" "v1.0" "SANBOX Manual"
.SH NAME
sanbox \- Lightweight OS-level sandbox manager using bubblewrap
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sanbox new
.I name
.br
.B sanbox
[
.I command
[
.I args...
]
]
.br
.B sanbox
{
.B \-h
|
.B \-\-help
}
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sanbox
is an ultra-lightweight, daemonless OS-level sandboxing utility built on top of
.BR bwrap (1).
It provides an isolated filesystem, network, and process space for developers and security researchers to run untrusted software, malware analysis, or isolated environments.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B new \fIname\fR
Create and initialize a new sandbox environment in a directory named \fIname\fR.
This directory will contain:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B config
- Configuration options for the sandbox (e.g. system mode, persistent paths).
.IP \(bu 2
.B root/
- The root filesystem mount point inside the sandbox where file writes are saved.
.IP \(bu 2
.B home/
- The isolated home directory for the sandbox.
.IP \(bu 2
.B loot/
- A default writable directory for persistent shared data.
.RE
.TP
.B [command [args...]]
Activate the sandbox from the current directory (which must contain a \fBconfig\fR file). If a command and arguments are provided, they will be executed within the sandbox. If no command is specified, it runs the default shell (\fB/bin/bash\fR if available, falling back to \fB/bin/sh\fR).
.SH CONFIGURATION
Each sandbox directory contains a \fBconfig\fR file sourced by the script. It supports the following variables:
.TP
.B SYSTEM
Determines the filesystem mode.
.RS
.TP
.B ro
Mounts the host's system binaries (\fB/usr\fR, \fB/bin\fR, \fB/sbin\fR, \fB/lib\fR, \fB/lib64\fR) as read-only. Recommended for general usage.
.TP
.B isolated
Provides a completely empty namespace with no host system binaries. Useful for static binaries.
.RE
.TP
.B WORDLISTS
Can be set to \fByes\fR or \fBno\fR. If \fByes\fR, bind-mounts common pentesting wordlist directories like \fB/usr/share/wordlists\fR and \fB/usr/share/dict\fR (if they exist on the host) read-only inside the sandbox.
.TP
.B RW_PATHS
A comma-separated list of directories (relative to the sandbox root) that should be writable and persistent (e.g. \fB/loot\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
Create and start a new sandbox:
.IP
.nf
$ sanbox new mybox
$ cd mybox
$ sanbox
[mybox] ~ #
.fi
.PP
Run a command directly inside the sandbox:
.IP
.nf
$ sanbox python3 exploit.py
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bwrap (1)
